 torn clothes a tricycle a lunch  never eaten photographs of burn victims  hang on the walls  next to artwork by survivors of hot  melting skin  and black radioactive rain through her  own artwork tonica still  processes that fateful day 75 years ago  the united states bombed your city do  you have any  criticism for america we were full of  rage  at first but once we saw the americans  it was clear they were just like  us years later she even met clifton  truman  daniel grandson of president harry  truman who ordered the bombing  there will be a time in the future when  there are  no more surviving hiroshima victims what  do you want the world to remember  eliminating nuclear weapons is the path  to peace  ensuring this tragedy is never repeated  and tonica manages  one last line in english please make  many friends from  other countries when you do so you are  moving the world towards peace  a remarkable woman who survived a  nuclear bombing  yet still has peace in her heart for cbs  this morning saturday  raymie innocencio hiroshima japan  sounds pretty good trying to meet many  different people from many different  countries  yeah and there was a long period of time  she didn't want to talk about it she  said because it was so painful but  realize how important it was  to speak out and important to remember  as raymie points out there will be a  time not too far away when no survivors  are left so  exactly really interesting to hear from  her
 

